Organophosphonate based nerve agents are amongst the most toxic compounds known to mankind. Their toxicity arises from their easy penetration through human mucosa and ulterior damage of the central nervous system by the inhibition of acetylcholinesterase (AChE) enzyme1. Some examples of nerve agents include soman (GD), tabun (GA) and VX. Although declared illegal by international agreements2, recent attacks with chemical weapons (CWA) against civil and military populations have been reported3,4. Consequently, protection and decontamination of these toxic chemicals is a very important societal challenge. Their hydrolytic degradation under environmental conditions (room temperature and ambient moisture) is one of the most convenient detoxification pathways, however, it will only take place in the presence of a suitable catalyst.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are being thoroughly studied for CWA detoxification as a consequence of their high porosity and functional tailorability5-8. Among them, zirconium MOFs based on $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ clusters have shown to be highly active in the hydrolysis of simulan $9^{-14}$ and real nerve agents15-17 as a consequence of the suitable combination of highly Lewis acidic zirconium metal centers and basic hydroxide/oxide groups.
The accessibility of the organophosphonates to the active metal clusters is determined by the pore size and connectivity of the MOFs. In this regard, MOF- $\mathbf{808^{18}}$ based on the tritopic benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (btc) linker and 6 connectivity with $1.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $2.2\ \mathrm{\nm}$ pore sizes and NU100019 based on the tetratopic $4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -(pyrene-1,3,6,8- tetrayl)tetrabenzoate linker and 8 connectivity exhibiting a hierarchical micro-mesoporous structure with $\mathbf{1.3\nm}$ and 3.4 nm pore sizes, are ideally suited for the detoxification of these chemicals. The high accessibility to the active catalytic sites of these systems leads to almost instantaneous degradation of nerve agents (i.e. GD and VX), under Nethylmorpholine basic buffer15,17. Nevertheless, the absence of the buffer leads to diminished activity20, as a probable consequence of nerve agent degradation products anchoring to the oxohydroxometal cluster14. This problem can be overcome by buffer heterogenization by formation of polymeric organic amine/MOF composites21,22 and/or doping with lithium alkoxides23,24 leading to highly efficient detoxification catalysts, in unbuffered aqueous solutions. Organic amines toxicity or lithium alkoxides air sensitivityprompt the search of alternative catalysts able to work under real environmental conditions.
In this report, we show that the pore size and distribution in UiO-66 (microporous), MOF-808 (mesoporous) and NU1000 (hierarchical micro/mesoporous) materials determine the reactivity towards the bulky reagent $\mathrm{\small{[Mg(OMe)_{2}(M e O H)_{2}]_{4}}}$ and their detoxification performance (Figure 1).
UiO-66, MOF-808 and NU-1000 were prepared according to reported synthetic methods18, 19, 25. The activated MOF materials were treated at room temperature with magnesium methoxide methanolic solutions at 1:1, 1:2 and 1:4 ${\mathrm{Zr}}_{6}{:}{\mathrm{Mg}} $ molar ratios to give rise to $\mathbf{MOF@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ 1:n materials (Figure 1, SI). Characterization $\mathrm{ofMg(OMe)}_{2}$ doped MOFs shows that crystallinity and porosity of UiO-66 remain unaltered (Figures $S2-3$ ). By contrast, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ isotherms of MOF808 and NU-1000 systems show a decrease of the second step (mesopore region) upon increasing loadings of ${\bf M g}({\bf O M e})_{2}$ (Figures 2a,b, $\mathrm{~}S_{3}\mathrm{~}^{\prime}$ ). Density functional theory (DFT) analysis ofpore size distribution agrees with a diminution of accessible mesopore volume for MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ and $\mathbf{NU-10oo0@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ systems (Figure 2c, d). MOF-808 exhibits adual pore distribution of $1.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ (pore windows) and 2.2 nm (pore voids). For MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ 1:1 and 1:2 systems we observe a decrease in accessibility of the 2.2 nm pore voids with MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ 1:4 system exhibiting a mainlymonotonic $\mathbf{1.8\:nm}$ pore size distribution (Figure 2c, S3b).
For NU-1000 we also observe the maintenance of the micropore volume along with a diminution  of mesopore accessibility  for $\mathbf{NU-10oo\@Mg(OMe)_{2}1:1}$ and 1:2 with total loss of mesoporosity for $\mathbf{NU-1ooo@Mg(OMe)_{2}1:}4$ (Figure 2b, d). Monte Carlo molecular modelling calculations26 were carried out to assess the accessibility and most probable adsorption configurations of $\left[\mathrm{Mg(OMe)_{2}(M e O H)_{2}}\right]_{4}$ $(\mathbf{1.3\nm\x}$ $1.3\ \mathrm{nm})^{27}$ into the pore structure of UiO-66, MOF-808 and NU-1000. The results show that this reagent is unable to enter the micropore structure of UiO-66 while it is selectively incorporated into the mesopores of MOF-808 and NU-1000 (Figure S20). SEM-EDX analyses of MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ and $\mathbf{NU-10oo0@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ are indicative of a highly homogeneous distribution of magnesium in the MOF particles (Fig. 2e-h). XPS analyses (Figure S6) agree with increasing amounts of magnesium upon ${\bf M g}({\bf O M e})_{2}$ loading into the MOF- $\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ and NU1 $\mathbf{ooo@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ systems up to $\mathbf{0.64}$ atoms of $\mathbf{Mg}$ per $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ metal cluster. $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR analyses of the digested doped systems show $50\%$ loss of formate residues for MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ as well as the absence of methoxide groups for both $\mathbf{MOF-8o8@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{NU-10oo0@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ (Figure S4). The latter fact is indicative of the actual conversion of $\mathbf{Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ on the pore surface of the materials.
In order to better understand the precise location of the magnesium centers within the ${\bf{Z r-M O F}}$ systems, Zr K-edge EXAFS were collected at $77\mathrm{K}$ on UiO-66 and MOF-808 before and after the treatment with the magnesium alkoxide. The results show that UiO-66 remains unaltered while for MOF-808 significant changes are noticed (Figures 3, S7-S11). EXAFS data collected on MOF- $\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ evidence a significant decrease in the intensity of the $\chi(\mathbf{r})$ -signal at ${\sim}3.1$ Å which corresponds to the $3{\cdot}54\ \mathrm{\stackrel{\circ}{A}}\ \mathrm{Zr}{\cdots}\mathrm{Zr}$ distances within octahedral $Z\mathrm{r}$ -clusters, after application of appropriate structural model and phase correction (Figure 3). The intensity of this EXAFS signal is associated with the coordination number $(N)$ of $\mathrm{Zr}$ within the node – that is, number of neighboring $\mathrm{Zr}$ atoms at a distance ${\sim}3{\cdot}54\mathrm{~\AA~}$ per each Zr. For a regular octahedral $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ -node within pristine MOF-808, $N=4$ . For a structural model in which $\mathbf{Mg}$ is attached to the $\mathbf{Zr}_{6}$ -node on the second-shell via hydroxyl/aqua groups, $N$ would remain unaltered. Fitting of $N$ values are indicative of a decrease from $_{3\cdot7}~\pm~0.6$ for pristine MOF-808 to $_{2.6~\pm~0.4}$ for $\mathbf{MOF-8o8@9Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ . These results are closely related to recently reported $\mathsf{Z r}_{6-\mathrm{x}}\mathsf{C e}_{\mathrm{x}}$ heterometallic clusters in UiO- ${\bf66^{28}}$ and point to the actual formation of $\mathrm{Mg_{x}Z r_{6-x}}$ heterometallic clusters. EXAFS data can be fitted using an octahedral $\mathbf{Mg}\mathbf{Zr}_{5}$ heterometallic cluster (Figures S10-11), demonstrating the simultaneous occurrence of $\mathbf{Mg}{\cdots}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}$ and $\mathrm{Zr{\cdots}Z r}$ distances at $3{\cdot}34\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and $3{\cdot}56$
Å, respectively. Moreover, the decrease in the signal centered at ${\bf-1.\dot{6}^{\mathrm{~\AA~}}}$ , which is associated with $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ (formates) bond distances at $2.28\pm\ 0.08\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ can be attributed to partial loss $(50\%)$ of formate groups upon ${\bf M g}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ doping (see above).
The effect of the formation of $\mathrm{MgZr_{5}O_{2}(O H)_{6}}$ heterometallic clusters on the SBUs of MOF- $\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ and NU$\bf{1000}@\bf{M g}(\bf{O M e})_{2}$ on the catalytic activity was evaluated against the hydrolytic degradation of the nerve agent model diisopropylfluorophosphate (DIFP) using a MOF:simulant 1:2 ratio, at room temperature, in unbuffered aqueous media (Figure 4). The results show a significant enhancement of catalytic activity of MOF-808 upon doping with $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ . Indeed, the material MOF-8 $\mathbf{\sigma_{0}8@M g(O M e)_{2}}$ formed with the addition of 4 equivalents of $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ , exhibits a fast degradation of the DIFP $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\ 6.8\ \mathrm{min}\right.$ , TOF $\mathbf{o.1\min^{-1}}$ ) with ca. 10 folds enhancement of activity compared to pristine MOF808 $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}~43~\mathrm{min}\right.$ , TOF 0.01 min-1) (Figures 4a, S16 and Table $S_{3})$ . For pristine NU-1000 (Figure $\mathbf{4b}$ and Table $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ) we observe an initial fast detoxification which is followed by reaction halting at ca. 90 $\%$ of DIFP degradation, indicative of catalyst poisoning as a consequence of a probable anchoring of DIFP degradation products to $Z\mathbf{r}$ -SBUs. By contrast, $\mathbf{NU-10oo}\ @\mathbf{Mg}(\mathbf{O}\mathbf{Me})_{2}$ gives rise to a steady and fast degradation of DIFP $\left(\mathrm{t}_{1/2}~2.7~\mathrm{min}\right.$ , TOF $\mathbf{o.26\min^{-1}},$ ) with 100 $\%$ detoxification after $35\ \mathrm{\min}$ (Figure $_{4\mathrm{b}}$ and Table $S_{3}$ ). As expected, treatment of microporous UiO-66 with $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ does not increase of the reaction rate (Figure S12, Table $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ). Filtration tests are indicative of the heterogenous nature of the catalytic process (Figure S14). Moreover, the catalytic activity of $\mathbf{MOF-8o8@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ remains unaltered during long storage periods while UiO-66@LiOtBu23 catalytic activity notably decreases in a 30-days timeframe (Figure S12), which agrees with the benefit of $\mathbf{Mg}$ incorporation into the MOF SBUs in comparison with LiOtBu doping taking place on its periphery.29 $\mathsf{p H}$ measurements for the MOF catalysts suspensions before the DIFP degradation process (Table $S_{5}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ) are indicative that the doped materials possess a moderate basicity: pH 4.1 for pristine NU-1000 vs pH 7.7 for $\mathbf{NU-10oo0@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ . However, the buffering effect of the $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ doping is limited with the $\mathsf{p H}$ values after the degradation process lying close to the values of pristine material under unbuffered conditions $_{(3.6-5.3)}$ (Table $S_{5}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ). Moreover, plain ${\bf M g}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ solution (initial $\mathrm{pH}=\mathbf{10})$ ) gives rise to a limited hydrolysis of DIFP (Figure S12 and Table $S_{3}$ ).
We have also essayed the catalytic performance of the materials in N-ethylmorpholine buffered solutions (pH value 0f 9.2).  The results show that pristine MOF-808 and MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ 1:1 and 1:2 materials are very active under these conditions, outperforming the results in unbuffered aqueous media (Table $S_{4}$ , Figure S17). This behavior is attributed to the large excess of $\mathsf{N}$ -ethylmorpholine which acts both as sacrificial base and nucleophile. The observed diminished activity for $\mathbf{MOF8o8@Mg(OMe)}_{2}\mathbf{1}{:}4$ system can be related to the lower pore accessibility of this material.
The results with DIFP simulant prompted us to study the degradation of real nerve agents GD and VX in unbuffered aqueous solutions. The experiments are indicative of a clear improvement on P-F and P-S bonds cleavage upon MOF doping with ${\bf M g}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ (Figures 5, S18 and Table $S_{3}$ ). Doped MOF- $\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ $\left(\mathbf{t_{1/2}}\mathbf{u}.6\mathbf{\mathrm{min}}\right.$ , TOF $\mathbf{o.o6\min^{-1}}$ ) exhibits a better performance towards P-F hydrolysis in the GD degradation (MOF:GD 1:2 ratio) compared to MOF-808 $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right.$ 34.7 min, TOF 0.02 min-1) (Figure 5a). Noteworthy, MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ materials gives rise to instantaneous hydrolysis of the P-S bond of VX (MOF:VX ratio 1:4) in 1:1 water ethanol mixture whereas pristine MOF-808 $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right.$ 5.0 min, TOF 0.14 min-1) possesses a moderate activity in unbuffered solutions (Figure ${\displaystyle5\mathrm{b}}_{,}^{,}$ ).
For NU-1000 the detoxication results are similar although somewhat more moderate. Indeed, GD degradation by NU$\scriptstyle\mathbf{1000}@\mathbf{Mg}(\mathbf{OMe})_{2}$ 1:4 $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right.$ 15.7 min, TOF $\mathbf{o.o44}\:\:\mathrm{\min^{-1}},$ outperforms NU-1000 $(\mathrm{t}_{1/2}~53~\mathrm{min}$ , TOF 0.013 $\mathrm{min^{-1}},$ ). In the case of VX the results show a closely related behavior of all materials with NU-1000@ $\mathbf{\nabla})\mathbf{Mg}(\mathbf{OMe})_{2}$ 1:2 $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right.$ 10.1 min, TOF $\mathbf{0.068\min^{-1}}$ ) slightly outperforming pristine NU-1000 $\left(\mathbf{t}_{1/2}\right.$ 19 min, TOF $\mathbf{o.o363}~\mathbf{min^{-1}},$ ) (Figure S18). These results are indicative that the improvement of NU-1000 activity upon ${\bf M g}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ doping seems to be more limited compared to MOF-808 material. The different behavior of MOF$\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ and $\mathbf{NU-10oo0@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ can be explained on the basis of the extent of $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ doping on both systems. As above mentioned, Monte Carlo computational modelling for the incorporation of both the doping agent and the toxic molecules on the pore structure of MOF-808 shows that both reagent and toxic molecules are located in the mesopore cavities (Figures $S_{20^{-}S22})$ ). By contrast, NU-1000 doping agent is incorporated exclusively onto the mesopore channels while toxic molecules can diffuse through both micropores and mesopores (Figures S20-S22). Consequently, for MOF- $\mathbf{8o8@Mg(OMe)_{2}}$ the toxic molecules are fully exposed to $\mathrm{Mg}\mathrm{Zr}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{6}$ SBUs of mesoporous cavities while for $\mathbf{NU-10oo0@Mg(OMe)}_{2}$ the lower extent of magnesium doping gives rise to a lower exposition to $\mathrm{MgZr}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{6}$ SBUs with a concomitant lower benefit of $\mathbf{Mg}(\mathrm{OMe})_{2}$ functionalization. We believe that the increased basicity (increased nucleophilicity of ${\mathrm{OH}}^{-}/\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ residues) and charge gradients in the $\mathrm{Mg}{\mathrm{Zr}}_{5}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{6}$ heteronuclear cluster gives rise to a synergistic effect affording polar P-X bonds $\mathrm{(X=F_{\cdot}}$ , OR, SR) hydrolitic cleavage. Summarizing, the different accessibility of the UiO-66, MOF-808 and NU-1000 pore structures determine both their reactivity towards $\left[\mathrm{Mg(OMe)_{2}(M e O H)_{2}}\right]_{4}$ and their detoxification properties.
